1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,157
For my project on invertebrates, I chose to study tardigrades.

2
00:00:04,157 --> 00:00:09,780
These are microscopic – or to be more precise – near-microscopic animals.

3
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:14,833
There are well over a thousand known species of these tiny animals,

4
00:00:14,833 --> 00:00:17,360
which belong to the phylum Tardigrada.

5
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:24,948
Most tardigrades range in length from 0.05 to 1 millimetre, though the largest species

6
00:00:24,948 --> 00:00:29,500
can grow to be 1.2 millimetres in length.

7
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:32,239
They are also sometimes called ‘water bears’:

8
00:00:32,239 --> 00:00:35,370
‘water’ because that’s where they thrive best,

9
00:00:35,370 --> 00:00:38,500
and ‘bear’ because of the way they move.

10
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:44,217
‘Moss piglet’ is another name for tardigrades because of the way they look when

11
00:00:44,217 --> 00:00:45,850
viewed from the front.

12
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:52,443
They were first discovered in Germany in 1773 by Johann Goeze,

13
00:00:52,443 --> 00:00:55,440
who coined the name Tardigrada.

14
00:00:55,440 --> 00:01:01,566
As I say, there are many different species of tardigrade – too many to

15
00:01:01,566 --> 00:01:07,220
describe here – but,generally speaking, the different species share similar physical traits.

16
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:10,377
They have a body which is short,

17
00:01:10,377 --> 00:01:16,690
and also rounded – a bit like a barrel – and the body comprises four segments.

18
00:01:16,690 --> 00:01:22,398
Each segment has a pair of legs, at the end of which are between four

19
00:01:22,398 --> 00:01:23,920
and eight sharp claws.

20
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:28,771
I should also say that some species don’t have any claws;

21
00:01:28,771 --> 00:01:30,792
what they have are discs,

22
00:01:30,792 --> 00:01:33,622
and these work by means of suction.

23
00:01:33,622 --> 00:01:39,281
They enable the tardigrade to cling on to surfaces or to grip its prey.

24
00:01:39,281 --> 00:01:42,110
Within the body, there are no lungs,

25
00:01:42,110 --> 00:01:44,940
or any organs for breathing at all.

26
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:51,858
Instead, oxygen and also blood are transported in a fluid that fills the cavity

27
00:01:51,858 --> 00:01:53,340
of the body.

28
00:01:53,340 --> 00:01:58,694
As far as the tardigrade’s head is concerned, the best way I can describe

29
00:01:58,694 --> 00:02:02,620
this is that it looks rather strange – a bit squashed even，

30
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:08,107
though many of the websites I looked at described its appearance as cute,

31
00:02:08,107 --> 00:02:10,640
which isn’t exactly very scientific.

32
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:17,248
The tardigrade’s mouth is a kind of tube that can open outwards to reveal

33
00:02:17,248 --> 00:02:19,450
teeth-like structures known as ‘stylets’.

34
00:02:19,450 --> 00:02:24,190
These are sharp enough to pierce plant or animal cells.

35
00:02:24,190 --> 00:02:27,040
So, where are tardigrades found?

36
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:32,814
Well, they live in every part of the world, in a variety of habitats:

37
00:02:32,814 --> 00:02:36,113
most commonly, on the bed of a lake,

38
00:02:36,113 --> 00:02:40,650
or on many kinds of plants or in very wet environments.

39
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:47,570
There’s been some interesting research which has found that tardigrades are capable of surviving radiation

40
00:02:47,570 --> 00:02:49,300
and very high pressure,

41
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:57,172
and they’re also able to withstand temperatures as cold as –200 degrees centigrade,

42
00:02:57,172 --> 00:03:01,671
or highs of more than 148 degrees centigrade,

43
00:03:01,671 --> 00:03:03,920
which is incredibly hot.

44
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:09,122
It has been said that tardigrades could survive long after human beings have been

45
00:03:09,122 --> 00:03:13,580
wiped out, even in the event of an asteroid hitting the earth.

46
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:20,064
If conditions become too extreme and tardigrades are at risk of drying out, they

47
00:03:20,064 --> 00:03:22,380
enter a state called cryptobiosis.

48
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:28,923
They curl into a ball, called a tun – that’s T-U-N – by retracting their head

49
00:03:28,923 --> 00:03:29,796
and legs,

50
00:03:29,796 --> 00:03:35,030
and their metabolism drops to less than one percent of normal levels.

51
00:03:35,030 --> 00:03:38,471
They can remain like this until they are re-introduced to water,

52
00:03:38,471 --> 00:03:42,850
when they will come back to life in a matter of a few hours.

53
00:03:42,850 --> 00:03:50,730
While in a state of cryptobiosis, tardigrades produce a protein that protects their DNA.

54
00:03:50,730 --> 00:03:58,019
In 2016, scientists revived two tardigrades that had been tuns for more than 30

55
00:03:58,019 --> 00:03:58,540
years.

56
00:03:58,540 --> 00:04:07,477
There was a report that, in 1948, a 120-year-old tun was revived,

57
00:04:07,477 --> 00:04:12,690
but this experiment has never been repeated.

58
00:04:12,690 --> 00:04:17,136
There are currently several tests taking place in space, to determine how long tardigrades

59
00:04:17,136 --> 00:04:19,042
might be able to survive there.

60
00:04:19,042 --> 00:04:21,900
I believe the record so far is 10 days.

61
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:24,090
So, erm, moving on.

62
00:04:24,090 --> 00:04:30,660
In terms of their diet, tardigrades consume liquids in order to survive.

63
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:34,420
Although they have teeth, they don’t use these for chewing.

64
00:04:34,420 --> 00:04:37,386
They suck the juices from moss,

65
00:04:37,386 --> 00:04:39,858
or extract fluid from seaweed,

66
00:04:39,858 --> 00:04:46,780
but some species prey on other tardigrades, from other species or within their own.

67
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:52,463
I suppose this isn’t surprising, given that tardigrades are mainly comprised of liquid

68
00:04:52,463 --> 00:04:55,710
and are coated with a type of gel.

69
00:04:55,710 --> 00:05:00,720
Finally, I’d like to mention the conservation status of tardigrades.

70
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:07,332
It is estimated that they have been in existence for approximately half a billion years

71
00:05:07,332 --> 00:05:11,740
and, in that time, they have survived five mass extinctions.

72
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:15,959
So, it will probably come as no surprise to you,

73
00:05:15,959 --> 00:05:21,866
that tardigrades have not been evaluated by the International Union for Conservation of Nature

74
00:05:21,866 --> 00:05:24,820
and are not on any endangered list.

75
00:05:24,820 --> 00:05:29,380
Some researchers have described them as thriving.

76
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:39,540
Does anyone have any questions they’d like to ask?
